# Orrery — Debug and automation surface

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Orrery carries a debugging and automation surface, `window.__orrery`, so the
game can be driven and inspected from code without moving a real pointer or
waiting on real time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and
the build installs the finished surface on `window.__orrery` as soon as the
game has initialized. Every scenario driven from code reaches the game
through it, so it is present and exactly as specified here.

Each operation is a read or a pose of the game's state. It arranges a
challenge and a machine, and the game's own editor rules, simulation, and
completion test run from there exactly as they do in play, so a scenario
driven from code behaves exactly like one played by hand.

The surface is inert during normal play, doing nothing until something calls
it.
{{else}}
Orrery carries a debugging and automation surface so the game can be driven
and inspected from code without moving a real pointer or waiting on real
time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and
the build's `initialize` returns the finished surface beside the state it
built, as the pair `[state, debug]`. The engine returns that same value from
`engine.debug`, and it is reached that way alone: nothing is installed on
the page. Every scenario driven from code reaches the game through it, so it
is present and exactly as specified here.

Each operation is a reading or a pose of `OrreryState`, written in the shape
of `update`, because the engine holds the state by value and nothing holds a
writable one. A pose takes the current state, as `DeepReadonly<OrreryState>`,
and returns the next `OrreryState`; a reading takes the state the same way
and returns what it read. A caller drives a pose through the engine's
`apply`, as `engine.apply((s) => debug.loadPuzzle(s, challenge))`, and a
reading against the engine's current state, as
`debug.snapshot(engine.state)`. A pose arranges a challenge and a machine,
and the game's own editor rules, simulation, and completion test run from
there exactly as they do in play, so a scenario driven from code behaves
exactly like one played by hand. Where the surface's implementation lives
under `src/` is yours to decide; `src/game.ts` declares and exports its
type, `OrreryDebugApi`.

The surface is inert during normal play, doing nothing until something calls
it.
{{/if}}

## A deterministic core

The surface rests on the simulation being deterministic: the run advances by
simulated cycles integrated against the delta time the game is given, so an
interval of game time reaches the same state however it was divided into
frames. Two more properties make it driveable from code:

- Render-free core. Game state advances from the elapsed time the game is
  handed, independent of a canvas, of the frame loop that measured it, and
  of wall-clock time. The dependency runs one way: the simulation reads
  nothing from the renderer. Collision is decided at the sample fractions
  `specs/simulation.md` fixes, never at rendered frames.
- No randomness. Nothing in this game rolls dice: the same machine on the
  same challenge produces the same run, cycle for cycle, on any machine at
  any frame rate.

## What the runtime provides instead

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The keyboard and the overlay belong to the runtime layer beneath the game,
and the surface carries no operation for the registered actions or for the
overlay. That layer delivers the actions the game registers from the key
bindings this specification fixes, and it draws and toggles the debug
overlay.

The clock is the exception, because nothing outside this build owns it. The
surface is what takes the game off real time and advances it, through
`setAutoStep` and `advance` below.
{{else}}
The clock, the keyboard, the pointer, and the overlay belong to the
{{engine.name}} engine, which documents them in the `engine/` directory at
the root of this repository, and the surface carries no operation for any of
them. The engine advances the game frame by frame and can take it off real
time, it drives the actions the game registers, it reports the pointer
position in the stage's logical units along with the press and release
edges, and it draws and toggles the debug overlay.
{{/if}}

What the overlay shows is Orrery's, under Diagnostics below.

The surface's pointer operations do not stand in for the player's pointer.
They feed the same input path it feeds, so a posed press and a player's
press are the same event to the game, and nothing is bypassed: hex, tray,
and tape targeting, drags, lays, and the focus rule all run exactly as they
do for a player.

## The operations

The surface carries `version` (`ORRERY_DEBUG_VERSION`, `1`), a plain number,
and the operations below. Values are plain numbers, strings, booleans, and
JSON data; challenges and solutions use exactly the formats of
`specs/formats.md`, and `x` and `y` are in the stage's logical units.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
{{else}}

Each operation's first parameter is the current state,
`DeepReadonly<OrreryState>`. A pose returns the next `OrreryState`, built
from the one it was handed, which it leaves as it was; a reading returns
what it read. The headings below name the parameters that follow the state.
{{/if}}

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `setAutoStep(enabled)`

Takes the game off real time, and gives it back. `setAutoStep(false)` stops
the frame loop advancing the simulation from the wall clock, so the game
changes only when `advance` says so. `setAutoStep(true)` returns it to
running itself, which is how a build starts and how it is played.

Drawing is unaffected either way: the loop keeps rendering, so the canvas
shows the state the most recent frame left.

### `advance(seconds, frames)`

Runs `frames` whole frames covering `seconds` of game time, each worth
`seconds / frames`, immediately and in order. `frames` defaults to `1`. Each
is a real frame, the same update the loop runs followed by a render, so the
game's own simulation and completion test produce the result and the canvas
reflects it.

A run advances by `SPEEDS[speed]` cycles per second of game time, so
`advance(1, 1)` and `advance(1, 60)` cover the same cycles and reach the
same outcome exactly; every cycle runs in full whatever the frame that
crossed it.

Advancing while the game is still stepping automatically adds to what the
wall clock is already doing, so a scenario that must be reproducible calls
`setAutoStep(false)` first.

### `reset()`
{{else}}
### `reset(state)`
{{/if}}

Restores every declared field of the game's state to its title-screen value:
the title screen with its first menu item highlighted, `mode` `campaign`, no
challenge open, an empty editor with empty histories, no run, the pointer
reported as up, `simTime` `0`, and all progress cleared, so nothing is
solved, every record and stash is empty, only the first campaign challenge
is unlocked, and both select screens land on their first row.

`muted` is deliberately untouched: muting is a player preference the runtime
owns.

Because it restores exactly the declared fields, any state of your own that
a frame depends on is derivable from them.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `snapshot()`

A pure read of the state, returned as the plain object under Snapshot shape
below. It changes nothing.

### `openChallenge(mode, index)`
{{else}}
### `snapshot(state)`

A pure reading of `state`, returned as the plain object under Snapshot shape
below. It returns no state, because it poses nothing.

### `openChallenge(state, mode, index)`
{{/if}}

Poses entering a challenge, with `mode` `"campaign"` or `"extras"` and
`index` from `0`. It does exactly what `confirm` on that row of that mode's
select screen does — opens the challenge in the editor, restoring any
stashed machine — and it acts from any screen: a live run is discarded and
an open challenge's machine is stashed first, exactly as leaving the editor
does. A locked or out-of-range row does nothing, exactly as it does for the
player.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `loadPuzzle(challenge)`
{{else}}
### `loadPuzzle(state, challenge)`
{{/if}}

Poses an arbitrary challenge: `challenge` is a challenge document in the
format of `specs/formats.md`. The game moves to the editor with that
challenge open, an empty machine, empty histories, and no run; the tray
derives from it exactly as it does for a shipped challenge, and
`challengeRef` reports it as loaded directly, so completing it touches no
progress or record. A document that is not well formed throws an `Error`
naming what is wrong with it, and changes nothing.

This is the surface's main lever: it puts the editor on a chosen challenge
without a course around it. A challenge posed this way is a challenge like
any other, and every rule of `specs/editor.md` and `specs/simulation.md`
applies to it unchanged.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `loadSolution(solution)`
{{else}}
### `loadSolution(state, solution)`
{{/if}}

Replaces the open challenge's machine with `solution`, a solution document
in the format of `specs/formats.md`, checked against the placement rules of
`specs/parts.md` as one whole. It empties both histories, clears the
selection and cursor, and leaves no run active. A solution that is illegal
for the open challenge, or a call with no challenge open, throws an `Error`
naming the first problem, and changes nothing.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `readSolution()`
{{else}}
### `readSolution(state)`
{{/if}}

A pure read: the current machine as a solution document, exactly what
`loadSolution` would accept to rebuild it. With no challenge open it throws
an `Error` saying so.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `challengeSolution(mode, index)`
{{else}}
### `challengeSolution(state, mode, index)`
{{/if}}

A pure read: the build's own reference solution for that challenge, as a
solution document, whatever is unlocked or solved. `specs/modes/campaign.md`
and `specs/modes/extras.md` require one per challenge; this is where they
are reachable. An out-of-range `index` throws an `Error` naming the bounds.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `pointerDown(x, y)`, `pointerMove(x, y)`, `pointerUp()`
{{else}}
### `pointerDown(state, x, y)`, `pointerMove(state, x, y)`, `pointerUp(state)`
{{/if}}

Report a press, a move, and a release at a logical stage position, feeding
the same input path the player's pointer feeds: targeting, selection, drags,
lays, and the focus rule all run as `specs/editor.md` and
`specs/controls.md` state.

**Each of the three takes effect immediately, when it is called, rather than
being sampled once per update.** The press, the move, or the release is
resolved in the state the call returns rather than deferred to the next
update.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
No frame need pass between them, so a whole machine can be placed from code
without advancing the game at all.
{{else}}
Each returns the state that press, move, or release left behind, and the
next call is handed that state, so a whole machine can be placed from code
without a frame passing between the calls.
{{/if}}

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `play()`, `step()`, `stop()`, `setSpeed(index)`
{{else}}
### `play(state)`, `step(state)`, `stop(state)`, `setSpeed(state, index)`
{{/if}}

The run controls, through the same code path the player's actions drive.
`play` and `step` do exactly what the `play` and `step` actions do in
`specs/editor.md`, refusal included; `stop` does what `back` does during a
run and nothing when none is active; `setSpeed` sets a live run's speed step
to `index`, clamped to `0` through `3`, and does nothing when no run is
active. A `step` completes its whole cycle
within the call, so its outcome, a fault included, is in the state it
returns.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
A typical scenario calls `loadPuzzle` to pose the challenge wanted,
`loadSolution` to stand a machine on it, `step()` repeatedly to run it a
cycle at a time, and reads the result from `snapshot()`.

## Snapshot shape

`snapshot()` returns:
{{else}}
A typical scenario applies `loadPuzzle` through
`engine.apply((s) => debug.loadPuzzle(s, challenge))`, then `loadSolution`
and `step` the same way to run the machine a cycle at a time, and reads the
result from `debug.snapshot(engine.state)`.

## Snapshot shape

`snapshot` returns:
{{/if}}

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen: "title" | "howto" | "select" | "editor",
  mode: "campaign" | "extras",
  menuIndex: <number>,
  selectIndex: <number>,
  howtoPage: <number>,
  campaign: {
    count: <number>,           // how many challenges the shipped course holds
    unlockedCount: <number>,
    solved: [<number>],        // ascending indices
    records: [{ cost, cycles, area } | null],  // one entry per challenge
    last: <number>,            // where the select screen lands
  },
  extras: {
    count: 10,
    solved: [<number>],
    records: [{ cost, cycles, area } | null],
    last: <number>,
  },
  challenge: {                 // the challenge open in the editor
    name: <string>,
    reagents: [<molecule>],    // exactly the formats of specs/formats.md
    products: [<molecule>],
    permitted: [<part kind>],
    target: <number>,
    source: "campaign" | "extras" | "custom",
    index: <number | null>,    // null when source is "custom"
  } | null,
  editor: {
    parts: [                   // placement order; the tape panel's row order
      { id, kind, q, r, rotation, length,
        cells: [{ q, r }] | null, closed: <boolean | null>,
        index: <number | null>, tape: [<instruction | null>] | null },
    ],
    cost: <number>,
    period: <number>,
    selected: <number | null>,
    focus: "field" | "tape",
    cursor: { part: <number>, col: <number> } | null,
    drag: {                  // the live drag, in one of its three shapes
      kind: "place",
      part: <part kind>, index: <number | null>,
      rotation: <number>, length: <number>,
      at: { q, r } | null,   // the targeted hex, null off every hex
    } | {
      kind: "move",
      part: <part id>,
      from: { q, r },        // the hex the press grabbed
      at: { q, r } | null,
    } | {
      kind: "lay",
      part: <part id>,
      end: "first" | "last",
    } | null,
    undoDepth: <number>,
    redoDepth: <number>,
  },
  sim: {
    status: "running" | "paused" | "faulted" | "complete",
    cycle: <number>,
    fraction: <number>,
    speed: <number>,           // the SPEEDS index
    motes: [
      { id, q, r,              // the hex at the last boundary
        x: <number>, y: <number>,  // the drawn position at the current fraction
        type: <mote type>,
        wheel: <number | null> },
    ],
    filaments: [{ a: <mote id>, b: <mote id>, weight: 1 | 3 }],
    poses: [{ part, rotation, length, cell: { q, r } }],
    grips: [{ part, spoke, mote }],
    tallies: [<number>],       // one entry per product
    area: <number>,            // distinct hexes banked so far
    fault: { kind, parts: [<part id>], motes: [<mote id>] } | null,
    metrics: { cost, cycles, area } | null,
  } | null,
  pointer: { x: <number>, y: <number>, down: <boolean> },
  muted: <boolean>,
  simTime: <number>,           // accumulated simulation time, in seconds
}
```

The shape is fixed, and every field is present whatever the screen and mode.
A field the current situation does not use reports its resting value rather
than going missing:

| Field | Resting value |
| --- | --- |
| `challenge` | `null` away from the editor |
| `editor` | present always; empty `parts`, `cost` `0`, `period` `1`, `selected` and `cursor` `null`, `focus` `"field"`, `drag` `null`, both depths `0` when nothing is open |
| `sim` | `null` while editing |
| `campaign.unlockedCount` | `1` |
| `campaign.last`, `extras.last`, `selectIndex` | `0` |
| `howtoPage` | `0` away from `howto` |

Every field is read straight off the game's state, so what the snapshot
reports is what the game holds. The derived ones are:

| Field | Derived from |
| --- | --- |
| `editor.cost` | `PART_COSTS` over the parts, as `specs/parts.md` computes it |
| `editor.period` | the tapes, as `specs/instructions.md` computes it |
| a mote's `x`, `y` | its hex, its motion, and `sim.fraction`, by the formulas of `specs/field.md` and `specs/simulation.md` |
| `sim.area` | the length of the area bank |

Three fields need the game to keep them honest every frame: `pointer`
mirrors the position and press the pointer input reports, `muted` mirrors
the runtime's mute bit, and `simTime` accumulates the delta time of every
update, whatever the screen.

## Diagnostics

The runtime's debug overlay shows the values the game registers with it as
diagnostic sources. Register at least the current `screen` and `mode`, the
open challenge's name and source, the part count and `cost`, the `period`,
the run's `status`, `cycle`, and `speed` when one is active, each product's
tally against the `target`, the mote count, the fault kind when there is
one, the focus, and the pointer position. Keep each one short enough to
read on a line, and keep every source a pure read, so watching the overlay
leaves the game as it is.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The overlay is part of the runtime layer you write. It draws the registered
sources, it is shown and hidden by the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code`
`Backquote`), and it reads the game without changing it.
{{else}}
Registering those values is the whole of Orrery's part, through
`InitApi.diagnostics`. Each source is called with the state current at the
read, as `DeepReadonly<OrreryState>`, and reads off that argument: a source
that closed over the state `initialize` built would report the title screen
forever, because every frame leaves a new value behind. Drawing the panel,
toggling it, and keeping it read-only are the engine's.
{{/if}}
